## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical debugging across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in a realistic CNPA flow, focusing on end-to-end latency and data correctness during policy changes.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end latency measurement across services
- Distributed tracing and per-tenant sampling
- Kafka producer/consumer offset lag and acks
- Transactional outbox and idempotent sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and read-after-write verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo transactional outbox
db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  await tx.insert('audit', auditRec);
  await tx.insert('outbox', { ...auditRec, event: 'AUDIT_LOG' });
  // commit happens here
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you distinguish tenant-specific lag from global system lag?
- What config changes to Kafka/SRE tooling help monitor and mitigate this without harming durability?

## Why This Is Asked

Observability is critical for CNPA; end-to-end tracing helps identify latency sources across HTTP, DB, and Kafka.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing
- OpenTelemetry SDKs and exporters
- Trace context propagation headers and Kafka headers
- Sampling strategies
- End-to-end verification under load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js OpenTelemetry setup sketch
const tracer = opentelemetry.trace.getTracer('cnpa');
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('http', { attributes: { 'http.method': req.method }});
  res.on('finish', () => span.end());
  next();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ensure producer retries don’t split traces?
- How would you visualize traces and correlate with latency metrics?
